{"t":1790322401,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"7cd8fc0a-603f-4681-93db-4b7fe3e67cbf","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-25T00:56:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 250056\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n856 PM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe Coastal Flood Warning in Alexandria was cancelled and\nreplaced with a Coastal Flood Advisory.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A Nor'easter brings below normal temperatures, breezy \n conditions, and gusty winds through this weekend. \n\n- 2) Warm and calm conditions next week before rainfall chances\n return by late next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Nor'easter brings below normal temperatures, breezy \nconditions, and gusty winds through this weekend. \n\nA Nor'easter will develop off the east coast this evening before \ntracking north towards New England. The proximity of the Nor'easter \nwill bring increased cloud cover, below normal temperatures, \nand breezy conditions to the area through the weekend. Surface \nhigh pressure to the west will maintain dry conditions locally, \nalthough the tight pressure gradient will result in gusty winds \ndaily. Cloud cover decreases overnight, with conditions \nfavorable for radiational cooling leading to a Frost Advisory \nbeing issued for the Alleghenies. While most of the area will \ndip into the 40s, higher elevations will see temperatures in the\n30s. Temperatures as low as 33 degrees in the Alleghenies will \nresult in frost formation. Below normal temperatures continue \nthrough the weekend with highs in the upper 60s to 70s and \novernight lows in the 40s and 50s.\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to show a good bit of uncertainty \nregarding the exact track of the Nor'easter as it tracks offshore. \nSome solutions have the track further south and west, leading to \ngustier winds and potentially increased precipitation chances. \nWhile model guidance trended westward overnight, models this \nmorning have backed off leading to continued uncertainty. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and calm conditions next week before rainfall \nchances return by late next week.\n\nAs this Nor'easter advances further offshore, an upper level ridge \nbuilds over the Great Plains bringing higher pressure, calmer winds, \nand predominantly dry condtions to our region. A few showers could \nlinger across northern Maryland Monday morning should this \nweekend's Nor'easter track further west. Mid-level ridging \nbuilding over the Midwest continues to raise daily temperatures \nthroughout the week, warming into above-normal temperatures by \nthe end of the week. \n\nA low pressure system advancing out of the Midwest brings humid \nconditions and chances for showers back to our region late next \nweek.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nVFR condtions are expected to continue across all terminals \nthis afternoon and through the TAF period. The biggest impact to\nterminals will be gusty winds as a Nor'easter tracks nearby. \nGusty north/northeast winds are expected through Friday, before \nshifting to north/northwesterly on Saturday. Each afternoon, \nwind gusts between 15 and 25 knots are expected across the \nterminals. \n\nCeilings improve this evening and into the overnight with limited \ncloud cover expected Friday and Saturday with high pressure located \nto the west.\n\nOn Sunday, other than a few showers lingering east of I-95, VFR \nconditions are expected across all terminals through the beginning \nof next week. Northeasterly winds persist, but the magnitude of \nwind gusts continue to be dependent on the track of the\nNor'easter.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSmall Craft Advisories are in effect for all waters through Saturday \nmorning with the exception of the lower portions of the Tidal \nPotomac and Chesapeake Bay. Here, a Gale Watch goes into effect\nFriday afternoon through early Saturday afternoon. Winds over \nthe waters remain out of the north/northeast gusting 20 to 35 \nknots. High end Small Craft Advisory winds or potential Gale \nforce winds continue through Saturday as the Nor'easter tracks \nnearby. If the Nor'easter were to track very close to the coast,\nGale conditions would be likely with additional Gale headlines \nneeding to be issued. \n\nNortheasterly winds persist through the beginning of next week. \nDepending on how close this weekend's Nor'easter tracks to the \nwest, SCA level gusts are likely to continue into Sunday before \nfinally weakening below SCA criteria on Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nA long duration coastal flood event within persistent onshore\nflow is underway. Coastal Flood Warnings are in effect for\nAnnapolis and Straits Point through Friday. Coastal Flood \nAdvisories are in effect for Alexandria, DC, Dahlgren, and \nSolomons Island through Thursday night. Further north along the \nBay (Baltimore northward) anomalies are forecast to be lower due\nto a significant northerly component of the wind.\n\nThe forecast gets very complicated later Friday into Saturday as\na coastal low strengthens and potentially retrogrades westward\ntoward the area. Model guidance shows an extreme amount of\nforecast spread with respect to tidal levels Friday afternoon\nthrough the weekend. This uncertainty is tied to uncertainty in\nthe placement of the aforementioned coastal low, and whether its\nassociated wind field will allow water to be able to start to\ndrain out of the mouth of the Bay. Further coastal flood\nheadlines may be needed during this time period, but it's also\npossible that water levels begin to decrease significantly\nstarting Friday night.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...Coastal Flood Advisory until 9 AM EDT Friday for DCZ001.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory until 2 PM EDT Saturday for MDZ008.\n     Coastal Flood Warning until 11 AM EDT Friday for MDZ014.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 6 PM EDT Friday for MDZ016-018.\n     Coastal Flood Warning until 5 PM EDT Friday for MDZ017.\n     Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Friday for MDZ509-510.\nVA...Coastal Flood Advisory until 6 PM EDT Friday for VAZ057.\n     Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Friday for VAZ503.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 10 AM EDT Friday for VAZ054.\nWV...Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Friday for WVZ501-505.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 2 PM EDT Saturday for ANZ530>532-\n     535-536-538>542.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 4 PM EDT Friday for ANZ533-534-537-\n     543.\n     Gale Watch from Friday afternoon through Saturday afternoon \n     for ANZ533-534-537-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...AVS/KJP/KVH\nAVIATION...AVS/KVH\nMARINE...AVS/KVH\n"},"forecast":{"t":1790322387,"days":{"2026-09-25":{"hi":73,"lo":57,"pop":16,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-26":{"hi":70,"lo":59,"pop":36,"short":"Slight Chance Light Rain"},"2026-09-27":{"hi":68,"lo":60,"pop":41,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-09-28":{"hi":72,"lo":60,"pop":5,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-09-29":{"hi":76,"lo":59,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-30":{"hi":79,"lo":62,"pop":8,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-01":{"hi":81,"lo":null,"pop":10,"short":"Partly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1790319600,"n":48,"ceil":[24700,24700,24600,24500,null,null,null,null,null,24900,24900,24900,null,null,null,24900,24900,24800,null,null,25000,25000,21900,18700,11000,10500,6400,5900,5400,4900,null,3200,2300,2000,1700,1400,1500,1500,1600,1700,1700,1800,2100,2400,2700,10000,10000,11000],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.4,8.9,8.3,8.1,7.8,7.5,7.3,7.1,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0],"spd":[9,10,10,11,11,11,11,12,12,13,13,13,13,12,11,10,8,7,9,9,9,10,10,10,11,11,12,13,13,12,13,14,15,15,14,14,14,13,13,13,12,12,12,11,11,11,10,10],"gst":[19,19,19,19,19,18,18